.6756635308265686,-0.019179487600922585,-0.46606677770614624,-0.0779392197728157,-0.01451751310378313,-1.7929627895355225,-0.266756147146225,-5.602820692729438e-06,-0.31233158707618713,-1.5282981395721436,-0.41718971729278564,-0.056067921221256256,-1.1920922133867862e-06,0.0,-0.0019382515456527472,-0.005096183158457279,-0.001898868940770626,-0.011868222616612911,-5.960462772236497e-07,-0.08303035795688629,-0.18058231472969055,-0.10542149841785431,-0.7852149605751038,-0.11147667467594147,-3.3378546504536644e-06,-0.16539497673511505,-0.0018512745155021548,-0.034245315939188004,-0.3921545445919037,-1.2806047201156616,-0.23134745657444,-0.253093957901001,-0.08117814362049103,-1.564137578010559,-1.3307597637176514,-0.750025749206543,-0.2073013186454773,-0.5193501114845276,-0.0002575780963525176,-0.2339821457862854,-0.0011449456214904785,-5.328513361746445e-05],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,32696,3,387,264,6351,1372,1741,429,400,22957,32696,91,284,220,16,4779,323,279,23606,198,78045,89,61,17,488,1147,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,59,60,4648,264,10526,49952,3704,400,89,2418,220,7379,57960,32696,488,1124,1975,1056,35702,32696,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\omega$ be a complex number such that $|\\omega| = 1,$ and the equation\n\\[z^2 + z + \\omega = 0\\]has a pure imaginary root $z.$  Find $\\omega + \\overline{\\omega}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c83d3dd7a072b28821f82dffb8a2373b7db9dda307b025ed1c97d43a681622c4"},"seconds":30.51102375984192,"seed":486032041942428319,"stop":"eos","text":"Given the quadratic equation \\(z^2 + z + \\omega = 0\\) with a pure imaginary root \\(z = bi\\), where \\(b\\) is a real number, we substitute \\(z = bi\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n(bi)^2 + bi + \\omega = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n-b^2 + bi + \\omega = 0\n\\]\n\nSeparating the real and imaginary parts, we get:\n\n\\[\n-b^2 + \\omega = 0 \\quad \\text{(real part)}\n\\]\n\\[\nbi = 0 \\quad \\text{(imaginary part)}\n\\]\n\nFrom the imaginary part, we see that \\(b \\neq 0\\), so the real part equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\omega = b^2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\omega| = 1\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n|b^2| = 1 \\implies b^2 = 1 \\implies b = \\pm 1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\omega = 1\\) or \\(\\omega = -1\\). We need to find \\(\\omega + \\overline{\\omega}\\). The complex conjugate of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(\\overline{\\omega}\\), and since \\(\\omega\\) is real, \\(\\overline{\\omega} = \\omega\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = \\omega + \\omega = 2\\omega\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\omega = 1\\), then \\(\\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\\).\n\nIf \\(\\omega = -1\\), then \\(\\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = 2 \\cdot (-1) = -2\\).\n\nHowever, since the problem does not specify which value of \\(\\omega\\) to use, we consider the possible values and note that the answer should be consistent with the properties of \\(\\omega\\). Since both \\(\\omega = 1\\) and \\(\\omega = -1\\) are valid, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\nThis is because the problem implicitly asks for the sum of \\(\\omega\\) and its conjugate, which is always \\(2\\omega\\) and in this case, the consistent value is \\(2\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
